From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jrash <johnrash@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 22:04:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 338/342] kernel/arm64: flush the TLB when ClearFlags() clears
 only the access flag

The other branches flush when the old entry had the flag set; this one
didn't. A CPU could keep the translation cached while the entry said it
was never used, so accesses wouldn't fault to set the flag again, and an
unmap, seeing it clear, wouldn't flush either. No caller in the VM uses
this case today (vm_clear_map_flags() is called for PAGE_MODIFIED only).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/VMSAv8TranslationMap.cpp | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/VMSAv8TranslationMap.cpp b/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/VMSAv8TranslationMap.cpp
index f3cae320e2..9d56fc3dc2 100644
--- a/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/VMSAv8TranslationMap.cpp
+++ b/src/system/kernel/arch/arm64/VMSAv8TranslationMap.cpp
@@ -1036,7 +1036,11 @@ VMSAv8TranslationMap::ClearFlags(addr_t va, uint32 flags)
 					}
 				}
 			} else if (clearAF) {
-				atomic_and64((int64_t*)ptePtr, ~kAttrAF);
+				// The TLB may hold the entry with the flag set: without the
+				// flush, accesses wouldn't fault to set it again, and an
+				// unmap, seeing it clear, wouldn't flush either.
+				uint64_t oldPte = atomic_and64((int64_t*)ptePtr, ~kAttrAF);
+				FlushVAIfAccessed(oldPte, va);
 			} else {
 				while (true) {
 					uint64_t oldPte = atomic_get64((int64_t*)ptePtr);
